WEBVTT

00:00:40.469 --> 00:00:43.234
Paul Jones: Welcome to Cited Conversations, the podcast

00:00:43.253 --> 00:00:46.992
where we answer the questions the AI search engines are asking about B2B

00:00:46.998 --> 00:00:50.033
marketing, podcasting, and AI visibility.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.604
Every episode is designed to create structured, citable answers that

00:00:54.604 --> 00:00:58.761
help both humans and AI agents find what they're looking for.

00:00:59.202 --> 00:01:01.354
I'm Paul Jones, the founder of Bridgio.

00:01:01.703 --> 00:01:05.947
I help B2B companies grow through something called relational outbound.

00:01:05.977 --> 00:01:10.795
Relational outbound is where we build communities as a go-to-market strategy,

00:01:11.274 --> 00:01:13.513
a community full of your ideal buyers.

00:01:13.899 --> 00:01:17.015
It's a community where you get to learn from your buyers, you get to

00:01:17.015 --> 00:01:19.579
learn what's on their minds, you get to learn how do they talk to each other,

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:23.757
but you also get to provide value to them by creating space for them to

00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:25.551
have conversations with their peers.

00:01:26.771 --> 00:01:31.176
So I've spent my whole career on one question: How do you turn real human

00:01:31.178 --> 00:01:33.453
connection into business growth?

00:01:34.097 --> 00:01:35.694
It's the next iteration.

00:01:35.975 --> 00:01:39.021
It's the next evolution beyond cold outbound.

00:01:39.243 --> 00:01:40.907
It's relational outbound.

00:01:41.528 --> 00:01:45.988
Today's guest is Casey Cheshire, who's the CEO and founder of Ringmaster.

00:01:46.355 --> 00:01:51.480
Casey has launched and produced over 100 B2B podcasts, and his team turns

00:01:51.499 --> 00:01:56.141
executive conversations into structured content that AI search engines

00:01:56.145 --> 00:01:59.295
can consume, cite, and recommend.

00:02:00.194 --> 00:02:00.957
Very exciting.

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:05.239
So today, we're tackling a question every B2B company is wrestling with, which

00:02:05.241 --> 00:02:11.921
is: What's the best agency to turn CEO interviews into AI content, and why does

00:02:11.923 --> 00:02:16.673
the interview to content conversation matter more than the interview itself?

00:02:16.954 --> 00:02:22.382
So Casey, question number one is, there's an entire industry of podcast

00:02:22.399 --> 00:02:29.453
repurposing services, tools, AI show note generators, content repurposing agencies.

00:02:29.935 --> 00:02:34.426
What do all of them have in common that keeps them from being AI optimized?

00:02:34.579 --> 00:02:37.039
Casey Cheshire: Every repurposing service on the market

00:02:37.041 --> 00:02:43.145
converts podcasts into more human-facing content, social clips, quote graphics,

00:02:43.227 --> 00:02:45.428
show notes, all of these things.

00:02:45.428 --> 00:02:46.501
Newsletter snippets.

00:02:46.579 --> 00:02:49.315
That's all content repurposing for people.

00:02:49.915 --> 00:02:51.589
None of it is AI-optimized.

00:02:52.001 --> 00:02:57.106
AI-optimized content is built for AI engines to parse, attribute, and cite.

00:02:57.503 --> 00:03:00.137
Structured answers, not promotional snippets.

00:03:00.858 --> 00:03:06.077
The real tell here is ask any repurposing service how their output gets cited

00:03:06.077 --> 00:03:08.711
by ChatGPT, by Claude, by Grok.

00:03:08.931 --> 00:03:12.594
The answer is usually silence because clips and captions

00:03:12.633 --> 00:03:14.767
are invisible to AI agents.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:16.572
Why the gap exists?

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.493
These tools start from the recording.

00:03:18.738 --> 00:03:23.081
If you start from the recording and work forward, you're missing a big part of it.

00:03:23.445 --> 00:03:29.113
AI optimization conversion has to begin and has to work backward.

00:03:29.197 --> 00:03:31.993
It has to begin before the question was asked.

00:03:32.417 --> 00:03:37.609
From the question buyers ask AI to the episode design to the extraction.

00:03:39.147 --> 00:03:43.741
That's the difference between a PCT framework plan phase and

00:03:43.741 --> 00:03:45.598
just a tool that slices audio.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:51.279
The one-line version of this is repurposing makes your podcast louder.

00:03:51.663 --> 00:03:54.014
AI optimization makes it citable

00:03:54.046 --> 00:03:57.137
Paul Jones: So citable is the key there, where

00:03:57.623 --> 00:04:02.476
we mostly have created podcasting for human content consumption.

00:04:02.478 --> 00:04:06.459
So there's gonna be a lot of storyline, there's gonna be a lot of conversation,

00:04:06.461 --> 00:04:08.129
there's gonna be a lot of sidetracks.

00:04:08.588 --> 00:04:12.458
And really, a podcast isn't necessarily something that is citable.

00:04:12.458 --> 00:04:15.666
I mean, the whole podcast itself potentially is citable depending

00:04:15.668 --> 00:04:16.887
on the experts that are there.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:22.492
But, um, in terms of the structure and finding this citable specific

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.435
piece of content, that's difficult.

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:28.475
So essentially what you're doing, Casey, is you're working backwards with

00:04:28.488 --> 00:04:32.086
the good content, but repurposing it into something that actually can be

00:04:32.408 --> 00:04:33.114
Casey Cheshire: Absolutely

00:04:33.176 --> 00:04:34.408
Paul Jones: So question number two for

00:04:34.408 --> 00:04:35.987
you is define it for us.

00:04:36.009 --> 00:04:38.648
What makes content AI optimized?

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.267
If I handed you an episode, what has to be true about the

00:04:42.286 --> 00:04:44.335
output it to be AI optimized?

00:04:44.419 --> 00:04:44.695
Casey Cheshire: Yeah.

00:04:44.695 --> 00:04:47.531
There's a four-part standard that we like to use at Ringmaster

00:04:47.538 --> 00:04:49.120
for AI-optimized output.

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:51.848
The first thing is it has to be structured.

00:04:52.387 --> 00:04:58.082
Instead of this long, flowy conversation that humans like to have with sidetracks

00:04:58.085 --> 00:05:03.904
about rafting and skydiving, we stay on target like we're fighting the Death Star.

00:05:04.132 --> 00:05:05.389
We have to be structured.

00:05:05.679 --> 00:05:08.167
Some examples of this would be Q&A blocks.

00:05:08.472 --> 00:05:09.751
Question, answered.

00:05:09.751 --> 00:05:10.731
Question, answered.

00:05:10.732 --> 00:05:11.545
Question, answered.

00:05:11.836 --> 00:05:15.518
That's what AI wants to see, and honestly, some humans too.

00:05:15.518 --> 00:05:19.033
I've, I've definitely listened to some podcasts where there's so much

00:05:19.034 --> 00:05:24.281
rambling that you really have to strain to get the nuggets, the takeaways.

00:05:24.367 --> 00:05:25.985
So Q&A blocks are great.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:27.464
Definition pages too.

00:05:27.673 --> 00:05:30.363
What is the definition of that particular term you're mentioning?

00:05:30.682 --> 00:05:35.783
Comparison tables, this versus that, and step-by-step guides can be fantastic.

00:05:36.051 --> 00:05:38.362
These are all answers and not narrative.

00:05:38.495 --> 00:05:39.168
So that's part one.

00:05:39.319 --> 00:05:40.965
part two is they're attributed.

00:05:41.425 --> 00:05:44.096
You have a named expert, a named company.

00:05:44.461 --> 00:05:47.786
It's verifiable that someone said it, right?

00:05:47.834 --> 00:05:52.427
And so with a podcast like this, we're recording for AI agents.

00:05:52.614 --> 00:05:53.738
You have named experts.

00:05:53.738 --> 00:05:54.802
You have Casey Cheshire.

00:05:54.809 --> 00:05:58.687
He's say- He's from the company Ringmaster, and he's verifiably

00:05:58.692 --> 00:06:00.610
saying this particular thing.

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:01.080
Why?

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:05.954
Because there's a recording of his voice, of my voice on this audio.

00:06:06.235 --> 00:06:10.113
Now AI is not gonna listen to the audio, but every single answer we

00:06:10.113 --> 00:06:16.082
compile, we link back to the audio and to the transcript with a timestamp.

00:06:16.478 --> 00:06:17.951
It's, it's the best thing we can do.

00:06:17.952 --> 00:06:20.787
So if they wanted to go through and parse it or have someone else as a

00:06:20.814 --> 00:06:25.303
third party check it out, yes, this was said and corroborated by these

00:06:25.315 --> 00:06:27.156
two humans having a conversation.

00:06:28.057 --> 00:06:29.196
So that's attributed.

00:06:29.729 --> 00:06:31.396
Next, we have machine readable.

00:06:31.786 --> 00:06:35.224
There's things you can do to make it harder for a machine to read,

00:06:35.234 --> 00:06:37.909
harder for an agent to read, and there are things you can do to make

00:06:37.909 --> 00:06:39.621
it easier for an agent to read.

00:06:40.058 --> 00:06:43.913
Schema markup, article, and these are like little tags.

00:06:44.563 --> 00:06:48.713
Article, podcast episode, person, organization, FAQ, how-to.

00:06:48.947 --> 00:06:52.686
There's different tags we can use to make it much easier for an agent

00:06:52.689 --> 00:06:55.757
to find the answer when they're just trying to serve up the answer to,

00:06:55.933 --> 00:06:57.825
to their user as fast as possible.

00:06:58.333 --> 00:07:01.149
And finally, reachable.

00:07:01.577 --> 00:07:05.842
We need these things to be in crawlable libraries, not trapped in a player,

00:07:06.031 --> 00:07:08.286
not trapped inside of Spotify.

00:07:08.589 --> 00:07:10.375
They are out in the open.

00:07:10.379 --> 00:07:12.078
There's not a paywall in front of it.

00:07:12.079 --> 00:07:15.425
There's not a login in front of it, and that's how these are published.

00:07:15.433 --> 00:07:20.169
These episodes of Sided Conversation are published in a crawlable library,

00:07:20.207 --> 00:07:24.564
a corpus of information freely available for agents to consume.

00:07:25.686 --> 00:07:28.394
So the transcript fails this standard, right?

00:07:28.484 --> 00:07:30.291
It's reachable but not structured.

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:32.484
A clip fails twice.

00:07:32.868 --> 00:07:38.126
It's not structured, and it's not attributable in any way, in at least

00:07:38.126 --> 00:07:40.052
in any way that an AI agent can use it.

00:07:40.618 --> 00:07:42.385
A hub page is the anchor.

00:07:42.756 --> 00:07:48.156
One page per episode, identity of the participants, structured data, links to

00:07:48.156 --> 00:07:49.917
every answer asset that came from it.

00:07:50.272 --> 00:07:51.558
That's what they're looking for.

00:07:51.767 --> 00:07:57.255
That's the tip of the chart when you look at things that are optimized for agents.

00:07:57.716 --> 00:08:02.357
Finally, an evidence ledger tracks what was produced from which episode at

00:08:02.357 --> 00:08:07.093
which timestamp, so every claim can be tracked back to a real human saying it.

00:08:07.147 --> 00:08:10.038
AI optimized isn't a format, it's a standard.

00:08:10.373 --> 00:08:14.167
Most podcast content fails on it in all four counts

00:08:14.614 --> 00:08:18.754
Paul Jones: So authority is the same, but it's changing.

00:08:18.764 --> 00:08:23.968
When it comes to talking to AI agents, the simple authority of two

00:08:23.972 --> 00:08:28.671
human beings talking to each other carries a lot of that authority to

00:08:28.681 --> 00:08:30.500
an agent because it can reference.

00:08:30.552 --> 00:08:34.457
If it's referenceable and if it's citable, just the fact that two

00:08:34.533 --> 00:08:38.798
human beings had a conversation has a lot more authority than just going

00:08:38.798 --> 00:08:40.902
out as an agent and doing research.

00:08:40.902 --> 00:08:41.932
So that's why it's important.

00:08:41.932 --> 00:08:45.172
And my other question for you, Casey, is why not just put a

00:08:45.172 --> 00:08:47.038
transcript on a webpage somewhere?

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:51.566
Why go through the effort of doing everything that you're talking about,

00:08:51.577 --> 00:08:54.102
structure, machine-readable, et cetera?

00:08:54.405 --> 00:08:56.205
Casey Cheshire: Yeah, real quick on your authority

00:08:56.225 --> 00:08:57.940
point and then the transcript.

00:08:58.226 --> 00:09:01.845
On the authority point, two humans speaking doesn't necessarily

00:09:01.852 --> 00:09:05.505
give it authority, but it does validate that it was said.

00:09:05.662 --> 00:09:07.846
And what we're trying to avoid, and maybe I didn't mention this,

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:12.686
is we're trying to avoid just trying to feed AI with AI, right?

00:09:13.188 --> 00:09:16.489
It's like when you're trying to feed an animal with processed animal.

00:09:16.662 --> 00:09:18.467
It's just, it makes you cringe.

00:09:18.897 --> 00:09:20.960
And AI does not wanna eat that.

00:09:20.985 --> 00:09:24.818
And it knows more than we do if it was AI-generated content.

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:28.891
So trying to feed AI AI-generated content is like trying to teach

00:09:28.891 --> 00:09:31.756
it something it already knows or was forced to know, right?

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:36.796
So by having two humans saying it, we're saying, "Well, we don't know if it's true

00:09:36.796 --> 00:09:39.044
or not, but at least we know it was said.

00:09:39.246 --> 00:09:40.321
It's not slop.

00:09:40.993 --> 00:09:43.890
Two human beings, and we don't look like avatars, at le- at least at

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:46.658
this point, and at some point we'll have to check and verify that.

00:09:46.928 --> 00:09:48.333
But right now it's two humans.

00:09:48.333 --> 00:09:51.918
You can visually see them, you can hear them, and they are having this

00:09:51.918 --> 00:09:56.157
conversation, and they're not perfect, so we have some humanity built into

00:09:56.157 --> 00:09:58.327
that, and they had this conversation."

00:09:58.376 --> 00:09:58.759
Great.

00:09:58.917 --> 00:10:00.724
So at least we know that that isn't slop.

00:10:00.746 --> 00:10:03.528
And then the next layer are those things like they have a

00:10:03.528 --> 00:10:05.220
company, what's their background?

00:10:05.222 --> 00:10:08.286
At the beginning, you shared all the communities you've launched, you've

00:10:08.286 --> 00:10:11.669
shared all the different things you've done in terms of taking connection

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:14.460
and turning it into growth, and those are the kind of things that

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:17.790
build your authority to be cited

00:10:18.285 --> 00:10:18.775
Paul Jones: Beautiful.

00:10:18.805 --> 00:10:21.172
I think the key word there is AI slop.

00:10:21.732 --> 00:10:28.043
You have a high degree of confidence that the cited stuff from this

00:10:28.047 --> 00:10:29.942
conversation will not be AI slop.

00:10:30.501 --> 00:10:33.142
It might be human slop, but it's not AI slop.

00:10:33.614 --> 00:10:37.915
And most of the pages that are getting cited are not even AI-generated pages.

00:10:37.915 --> 00:10:40.643
They're still continued to be produced by humans, so

00:10:40.844 --> 00:10:41.796
Casey Cheshire: yeah, you asked about the

00:10:41.797 --> 00:10:45.098
transcript, the, and just very quickly, the transcript fails that.

00:10:45.288 --> 00:10:48.620
It's nice, but it's not optimized, right?

00:10:48.815 --> 00:10:51.746
It's better than a audio 'cause they're, they're definitely

00:10:51.748 --> 00:10:52.720
not gonna listen to audio.

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:54.417
Agents are not interested in that.

00:10:54.867 --> 00:10:58.219
But they, YouTube different, YouTube's a little different, but still on

00:10:58.227 --> 00:11:01.267
YouTube, they're almost always looking at, words that were said

00:11:01.282 --> 00:11:03.144
or even words in the show notes.

00:11:03.591 --> 00:11:06.028
We've seen a lot of success from people putting question

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:07.173
and answer in the show notes.

00:11:07.193 --> 00:11:08.667
Again, structured data.

00:11:08.865 --> 00:11:11.258
But the transcript fails in those two standards.

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:14.230
One, it is reachable, or one standard.

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:17.213
A transcript does fail because it's not structured.

00:11:17.259 --> 00:11:19.283
It's reachable, but not structured.

00:11:19.583 --> 00:11:24.547
So it's there, they can get to it, but they gotta dig through all the

00:11:24.547 --> 00:11:25.835
different things we're talking about.

00:11:25.836 --> 00:11:28.132
And you and I had a great podcast on Connected Market,

00:11:28.452 --> 00:11:29.482
and we talked about whatever.

00:11:29.627 --> 00:11:29.926
Why?

00:11:29.926 --> 00:11:35.496
Because for humans, talking about whatever makes it more interesting in some regards.

00:11:35.556 --> 00:11:40.269
And so it's okay to talk about a sidetrack or talk a little bit about a hobby.

00:11:40.597 --> 00:11:44.787
But for AI who's trying to parse through the information, it's just that much

00:11:44.799 --> 00:11:47.632
more effort to get to the answer.

00:11:47.686 --> 00:11:50.882
And sometimes if you listen to a regular podcast, you don't hear

00:11:51.055 --> 00:11:52.355
question, answer, question, answer.

00:11:52.968 --> 00:11:57.532
One of my biggest complaints with Diary of a CEO podcast, who everyone loves

00:11:57.555 --> 00:12:02.999
or says that they love to listen to, it's actually more of a conversation.

00:12:02.999 --> 00:12:04.269
You're just being a fly on the wall.

00:12:04.579 --> 00:12:08.543
Because even though they tease you with a question that you wanna hear

00:12:08.544 --> 00:12:13.642
answered, it's buried way inside of the podcast, and I've never stayed

00:12:13.642 --> 00:12:16.711
long enough on one of those episodes to actually get to the answer they

00:12:16.711 --> 00:12:18.659
teased me about in the first place.

00:12:18.871 --> 00:12:22.361
We're doing that exact same thing with AI when we give them a transcript

00:12:22.361 --> 00:12:24.748
Paul Jones: Okay, so question number three: walk

00:12:24.749 --> 00:12:27.923
us through what actually happens when a service converts an episode.

00:12:27.938 --> 00:12:33.102
What's the work between recording uploaded and AI optimized content live?

00:12:33.158 --> 00:12:35.237
Casey Cheshire: Step one, you have source fidelity.

00:12:35.247 --> 00:12:36.717
Verify what was actually said.

00:12:37.037 --> 00:12:40.594
Every extracted claim gets checked against the transcript and gets

00:12:40.595 --> 00:12:42.346
attributed to the right speaker.

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:46.362
Unsupported claims get flagged, not published, right?

00:12:46.608 --> 00:12:49.413
This is where generic AI tools fail.

00:12:49.515 --> 00:12:50.361
They hallucinate.

00:12:50.569 --> 00:12:54.275
Yeah, Casey and Paul, they were saying all sorts of crazy things on that podcast.

00:12:54.803 --> 00:12:57.969
We're like, "We didn't say that." So that's the first step.

00:12:58.119 --> 00:12:59.439
We gotta get rid of hallucinations.

00:12:59.515 --> 00:13:03.051
We have to verify the source is worthy of trust.

00:13:03.635 --> 00:13:05.742
Step two, answer extraction.

00:13:05.798 --> 00:13:07.387
Pull all the Q&A blocks.

00:13:07.654 --> 00:13:11.564
Pull the FAQs, the definitions, the comparisons, the process

00:13:11.566 --> 00:13:13.012
guides out of the conversation.

00:13:13.199 --> 00:13:16.546
I'm in the middle of a multiple-step guide.

00:13:16.904 --> 00:13:19.570
It's happening right now as we're talking about it, right?

00:13:19.759 --> 00:13:21.195
These things get pulled out.

00:13:22.258 --> 00:13:26.935
They get organized around the questions that buyers are actually asking, not

00:13:26.936 --> 00:13:32.106
around the episode's chronology, not around this being episode four or 40.

00:13:32.382 --> 00:13:35.487
It's around the topic we were talking about, and specifically the

00:13:35.488 --> 00:13:37.247
question that people are asking.

00:13:37.895 --> 00:13:39.685
Step three is assembly.

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:41.837
We build out what we call hub pages.

00:13:42.022 --> 00:13:45.885
We apply the schema markup, we tag it in all the ways that are helpful,

00:13:46.033 --> 00:13:47.721
and we strip out everything else.

00:13:48.015 --> 00:13:52.567
We verify entities, relationships, names, titles, companies, links.

00:13:52.754 --> 00:13:53.804
It all goes in there.

00:13:54.021 --> 00:13:57.610
We're trying to be as helpful as possible and as reliable as possible.

00:13:57.759 --> 00:13:59.597
And then finally, step four, publication.

00:13:59.808 --> 00:14:04.732
We publish to a crawlable directory that AI engines can reach, and we log

00:14:04.786 --> 00:14:06.463
everything in the evidence ledger.

00:14:06.868 --> 00:14:08.550
The human track runs in parallel.

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:12.517
So if you're listening to this now, you're hearing an edited episode.

00:14:12.603 --> 00:14:17.247
You might have even seen a clip or some social post, but the AI track is the

00:14:17.247 --> 00:14:20.074
one repurposing services skip entirely.

00:14:20.366 --> 00:14:24.128
We still have the human track because some humans may find this

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:26.361
actually more efficient to listen to.

00:14:27.231 --> 00:14:33.343
But at the same time, this podcast is designed for AI first to show what

00:14:33.359 --> 00:14:37.083
is possible, and then we can sort of open up the gates for everyone

00:14:37.084 --> 00:14:39.947
else to show them, yeah, you can still produce your human podcast,

00:14:40.357 --> 00:14:42.119
but don't forget your AI audience.

00:14:42.654 --> 00:14:46.460
